--TEST--
`break N` and `continue N` leave the N-th enclosing loop or `switch`, counted PHP's way — `rule:types/arithmetic`'s neighbours in the statement grammar, and `nvs_ir::lower::Lowering::lower_break`/`lower_continue`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/break-and-continue
// A level names an enclosing statement by count, outermost last: level 1 is
// the innermost, and every loop *and* every `switch` is one of them. Nothing
// below is a rendering of what Novis chose — the whole file is byte-identical
// to the same program under PHP, which is what `break 2` has to be.
array<string> $outer = ["a", "b", "c"];
array<string> $inner = ["x", "y", "z"];

// `break 2` leaves both loops at once, so neither the inner loop's remaining
// elements nor the outer loop's tail runs again.
foreach ($outer as string $o) {
    foreach ($inner as string $i) {
        if ($i == "y") {
            break 2;
        }
        echo $o, $i, "\n";
    }
    echo "outer-tail\n";
}
echo "after-break\n";

// `continue 2` ends the inner loop and starts the *outer* one's next
// iteration, so the outer tail is skipped every time while the outer loop
// still runs to its end.
foreach ($outer as string $p) {
    foreach ($inner as string $q) {
        if ($q == "y") {
            continue 2;
        }
        echo $p, $q, "\n";
    }
    echo "inner-done\n";
}
echo "after-continue\n";

// The row this exists for: a `switch` is a level, so `continue 2` written
// inside one is PHP's own idiomatic spelling for "continue the enclosing
// loop" — and it means exactly that here. `n=2` prints nothing and skips the
// tail; every other iteration runs both.
int $n = 0;
while ($n < 3) {
    $n = $n + 1;
    switch ($n) {
        case 2:
            continue 2;
        default:
            echo "n=", $n, "\n";
    }
    echo "tail=", $n, "\n";
}

// A level is bounded below as well as above: `break 1` is the innermost
// statement and means exactly what a bare `break` does.
int $m = 0;
while ($m < 9) {
    $m = $m + 1;
    if ($m == 2) {
        break 1;
    }
}
echo "m=", $m, "\n";

// The releases a multi-level jump owes are the intervening loops', not just
// its own: each iteration below binds two `string`s the jump abandons.
array<string> $keys = ["k1", "k2"];
foreach ($keys as string $k) {
    foreach ($inner as string $v) {
        string $joined = $k . "/" . $v;
        if ($joined == "k1/y") {
            break 2;
        }
        echo $joined, "\n";
    }
}
echo "done\n";
--EXPECT--
ax
after-break
ax
bx
cx
after-continue
n=1
tail=1
n=3
tail=3
m=2
k1/x
done
